  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DNA  BILD Tanzania - DNA Tanzania</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">DNA BILD Tanzania</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">I korthet syftar projektet att fr&#xe4;mja inkluderande, klimatsmart ekonomisk utveckling i mejerisektorn med fokus p&#xe5; akt&#xf6;rer med bristande resurser och
m&#xf6;jligheter, s&#xe4;rskilt kvinnor och ungdomar, l&#xe4;ngs med v&#xe4;rdekedjan f&#xf6;r mejeriprodukter</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In brief, the aim of the project is to to enable inclusive, climate-smart economic development in the dairy sector, with an emphasis on resource-poor, opportunity-constrained stakeholders, particularly women and youth, across the dairy value chain.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The DNA-BILD&apos;s overall objective is to contribute to transform Tanzania&apos;s dairy industry by creating vibrant ecosystems of farmer-allied and environmentally sustainable enterprises that improve nutrition, enhance livelihoods, and stimulate inclusive economic growth. The theory of change sets farmer-allied enterprises (FAEs), thus small-medium enterprises with strong links to farmers, to achieve long-term growth and to sustainably scale the benefits both upstream to farmers and farmer livelihoods, and downstream to improve nutrition of consumers. The enterprises are positioned to strategically and
intentionally supply and/or source from smallholder farmers in a way that strengthens their capacity, improves their productivity, and enhances their livelihoods, resiliency, and adaptation to climate change. This, in turn, will enable the production of affordable, locally produced dairy products improving the consumption of high quality nutritious and safely processed dairy. Furthermore, the project DNA-BILD will build on the learnings, expertise and working methods of the Dairy Nourishes Africa (DNA) consortium and expands the initiatives outreach to underserved populations, particularly women and youth, and enhances programming for climate mitigation and resilience.</narrative>
  </description>
